Excellent. You've reached what I consider **Version 1.0** of the review architecture.

You now have:

* ✅ Clean model (`Page`)
* ✅ Partial rendering
* ✅ HTMX endpoint
* ✅ HTMX navigation
* ✅ No page refreshes

From here, I think we should stop thinking of it as a "web page" and start thinking of it as a **desktop review application**.

---

# Commit 5

```text
feat(review): add keyboard navigation
```

This is, by far, the feature users will appreciate the most.

Once you've used left/right arrows to review 500 images, you'll never want to click buttons again.

---

# Philosophy

I **don't** recommend doing this with HTMX triggers.

Instead, use **15 lines of vanilla JavaScript**.

Why?

Because it's:

* simpler
* easier to debug
* easier to extend
* independent of HTMX internals

---

# Step 1

Create

```
web/static/js/review.js
```

Contents:

```javascript
document.addEventListener("keydown", function (event) {

    // Ignore typing in inputs
    if (
        event.target.tagName === "INPUT" ||
        event.target.tagName === "TEXTAREA"
    ) {
        return;
    }

    if (event.key === "ArrowRight") {

        const next = document.querySelector("#next-button");

        if (next && !next.disabled) {
            next.click();
        }
    }

    if (event.key === "ArrowLeft") {

        const prev = document.querySelector("#prev-button");

        if (prev && !prev.disabled) {
            prev.click();
        }
    }

});
```

No HTMX API.

Just clicks the buttons.

---

# Step 2

Give the buttons IDs.

Previous

```templ
<button
    id="prev-button"
    class="button"
```

Next

```templ
<button
    id="next-button"
    class="button is-primary"
```

Nothing else changes.

---

# Step 3

Load the script.

In

```
layout.templ
```

right after

```html
<script src="/static/js/htmx.min.js"></script>
```

add

```html
<script src="/static/js/review.js"></script>
```

That's it.

---

# Result

Now

```
←
```

clicks Previous.

```
→
```

clicks Next.

Since HTMX is already attached to the buttons,

the keyboard automatically performs HTMX requests.

No extra code.

---

# Why I prefer this

Many HTMX examples use

```html
hx-trigger="keyup[key=='ArrowRight']"
```

I don't.

Because six months from now you'll want

```
Space

Home

End

Delete

K

F

R

```

Using JS makes that trivial.

---

